# Experiment 076 — Calibrated Asymmetric Safe-Action Expansion

## Objective

Experiment 075 established that the dominant remaining responsiveness problem is a safe-action-set recall failure.

The primary learned gate achieved:

\[
\text{mean safe-action recall}
=
80.000\%
\]

with

\[
\text{mean safe-action precision}
=
96.889\%.
\]

The true responsive action was excluded in

\[
37.333\%
\]

of held-out contexts.

At the same time, downstream selection failure occurred in only

\[
1.333\%
\]

of contexts.

This established that the controller does not primarily need a better tie-breaker.

It needs a mechanism for recovering safe actions that the conservative primary gate excludes.

Experiment 076 therefore introduces calibrated action-level safe-set expansion.

The primary consequence gate remains unchanged.

For actions outside the primary safe set, a new action-level classifier estimates

\[
\hat p_t^{\text{safe}}(a)
=
P
\left(
a\in A_t^*
\mid
x_t
\right).
\]

An excluded action is added when

\[
\hat p_t^{\text{safe}}(a)
\geq
\tau.
\]

The expanded safe set is therefore

\[
\hat A_t^{\text{expanded}}(\tau)
=
\hat A_t^{\text{primary}}
\cup
\left\{
a:
\hat p_t^{\text{safe}}(a)
\geq
\tau
\right\}.
\]

The central question is

\[
\boxed{
\text{Can action-level expansion recover missed responsive actions}
\atop
\text{without admitting dangerous false-safe actions?}
}
\]

