## False-Safe Regret

The central limitation of probability-only expansion is revealed by the regret associated with false-safe actions.

At

\[
\tau=0.50,
\]

mean false-safe regret was

\[
0.050869.
\]

At

\[
\tau=0.60,
\]

it was

\[
0.049790.
\]

At

\[
\tau=0.70,
\]

it remained

\[
0.044001.
\]

At

\[
\tau=0.80,
\]

it was still

\[
\boxed{
0.040687.
}
\]

Across the active expansion thresholds, maximum false-safe regret remained

\[
\boxed{
0.095188.
}
\]

Therefore higher safety probability does not imply low downside when the prediction is wrong.

This is the decisive result of Experiment 076.

---

## Probability Is Not Enough

The expansion model asks

\[
P
\left(
a\in A_t^*
\mid
x_t
\right).
\]

This captures likelihood of safety.

It does not capture the magnitude of the mistake when an unsafe action is admitted.

Two candidate actions may have identical estimated safe probabilities while having radically different false-inclusion consequences.

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
P(\text{safe})
\text{ alone is not an adequate expansion criterion}.
}
\]

The expansion decision must become cost-aware.

---

## Recall–Precision–Regret Frontier

Experiment 076 demonstrates a three-dimensional frontier.

Lower thresholds increase:

\[
\text{recall}
\]

and

\[
\text{responsive retention}.
\]

But they reduce:

\[
\text{precision}
\]

and increase:

\[
\text{regret}
\]

and

\[
\text{under-persistence}.
\]

This means the safe-action problem cannot be optimized as a conventional binary classification task.

The relevant objective is asymmetric decision quality.

---

## Candidate Expansion Utility

For an excluded action \(a\), define:

\[
p_t(a)
=
P(a\in A_t^*).
\]

Now define the expected downside if it is not safe:

\[
d_t(a)
=
\mathbb{E}
\left[
R_t(a)
\mid
a\notin A_t^*
\right].
\]

A more meaningful expansion score would combine both quantities.

For example:

\[
U_t(a)
=
p_t(a)
-
\gamma
\left[
1-p_t(a)
\right]
d_t(a).
\]

Alternatively, expansion could require both:

\[
p_t(a)\geq\tau_p
\]

and

\[
\hat d_t(a)\leq\tau_d.
\]

This allows high-confidence but high-downside actions to remain excluded.

---

## Comparison With the Primary Gate

The primary gate achieves relatively low regret because it is highly conservative.

Its weakness is safe-action recall.

The expansion model demonstrates that this conservatism can be reduced.

However, removing it indiscriminately exposes the controller to a small number of severe errors.

Thus the goal is not simply:

\[
\text{increase recall}.
\]

It is:

\[
\boxed{
\text{recover safe false negatives while rejecting high-cost false positives}.
}
\]

---

## Structural Interpretation

Experiments 075 and 076 together now fully characterize the gate problem.

### Experiment 075

The gate is high-precision but low-recall.

Most responsive errors are false negatives.

### Experiment 076

Action-level probability models can recover those false negatives.

However, probability-only expansion admits rare but expensive false positives.

Therefore the remaining problem is not whether safe actions can be recovered.

They can.

The remaining problem is determining which expansions are worth the asymmetric downside risk.

---

## Principal Conclusion

Experiment 076 successfully demonstrates calibrated action-level safe-set expansion.

The expansion model can raise responsive-action retention from

\[
62.667\%
\]

to as high as

\[
96.000\%.
\]

At the more conservative operating point

\[
\tau=0.80,
\]

responsive retention improves to

\[
74.667\%,
\]

while recall rises from

\[
80.000\%
\]

to

\[
86.000\%.
\]

Nine previously excluded responsive actions are recovered with only two harmful expansion contexts.

However, mean regret still increases:

\[
0.003148
\rightarrow
0.004084.
\]

The reason is that false-safe actions, though rare, carry substantial downside.

At

\[
\tau=0.80,
\]

mean false-safe regret remains

\[
0.040687
\]

and maximum false-safe regret remains

\[
0.095188.
\]

Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{action-level safe probability improves recall}
}
\]

but

\[
\boxed{
\text{probability-only expansion is not sufficiently consequence-aware}.
}
\]

