## Next Research Direction

Experiment 077 should introduce cost-aware safe-action expansion.

For each excluded action, the controller should estimate both:

\[
\hat p_t^{\text{safe}}(a)
\]

and a downside estimate such as

\[
\hat d_t(a)
=
\widehat{
\mathbb{E}
\left[
R_t(a)
\mid
a\notin A_t^*
\right]
}.
\]

Expansion should then depend on both confidence and consequence.

A two-threshold rule could be:

\[
a
\in
\hat A_t^{\text{expanded}}
\]

only when

\[
\hat p_t^{\text{safe}}(a)
\geq
\tau_p
\]

and

\[
\hat d_t(a)
\leq
\tau_d.
\]

Alternatively, a unified cost-aware score could be defined as

\[
S_t(a)
=
\hat p_t^{\text{safe}}(a)
-
\gamma
\left(
1-\hat p_t^{\text{safe}}(a)
\right)
\hat d_t(a).
\]

Experiment 077 should evaluate whether cost-aware filtering can preserve the responsive-action recoveries achieved by Experiment 076 while eliminating the high-regret harmful expansions.

The primary metrics should include:

- safe-action recall,
- safe-action precision,
- responsive-action retention,
- recovered responsive contexts,
- harmful expansion contexts,
- mean and maximum false-safe regret,
- policy mean regret,
- under-persistence,
- over-persistence,
- action entropy,
- and expansion efficiency.

The central question becomes

\[
\boxed{
\text{Can the controller recover missed safe actions selectively}
\atop
\text{by accounting for both safety probability and false-inclusion cost?}
}
\]